course: space
lesson: 21
title: The night sky in summer and autumn, and the southern sky
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The sky changes with the seasons, because as Earth goes round the Sun our night side faces a different part of space.
explain: On summer evenings in Britain three bright stars, Vega, Deneb and Altair, make a huge triangle almost straight overhead: the Summer Triangle.
explain: In autumn the Great Square of Pegasus climbs in the east, and from its corner a line of stars leads to the misty smudge of the Andromeda Galaxy.
explain: Picture a cousin in Sydney and you in Leeds looking up on the same night: you see the Plough, she sees the Southern Cross, because you stand on different sides of the round Earth.
explain: People everywhere have made pictures of the sky: Aboriginal Australians see an Emu in the dark dust clouds of the Milky Way.
fact: Vega in Lyra, Deneb in Cygnus and Altair in Aquila form the Summer Triangle, high overhead on summer and early autumn evenings in Britain and North America.
fact: Around midsummer the sky over Britain never gets fully dark, and in Scotland it stays light enough to read outside near midnight.
fact: Cygnus the swan, also called the Northern Cross, flies along the Milky Way with Deneb at its tail.
fact: Scorpius, with red Antares, and Sagittarius, shaped like a teapot, lie low in the south in summer from Britain but much higher from the southern US and Australia.
fact: The centre of the Milky Way lies in the direction of Sagittarius, where the band of the galaxy is brightest and thickest.
fact: In autumn the Great Square of Pegasus rises in the east, and from its corner a line of stars leads to Andromeda.
fact: The Andromeda Galaxy, about 2.5 million light years away, is the farthest thing most people can see with the naked eye, a faint smudge in autumn skies.
fact: From Australia and New Zealand you see a different part of the sky, including stars never visible from Britain, such as the Southern Cross, while the Plough and Polaris are hidden.
fact: The Southern Cross, Crux, is the smallest of the 88 constellations.
fact: The Southern Cross appears on the flags of both Australia and New Zealand.
fact: There is no bright south pole star, so southern observers find south using the long arm of the Southern Cross and the two Pointers, Alpha and Beta Centauri.
fact: Aboriginal Australians see the Emu in the Sky in the dark dust clouds of the Milky Way.
fact: In New Zealand the rise of Matariki, the Pleiades, now marks a Māori New Year public holiday.
exercise: Which three stars make the Summer Triangle? | Vega, Deneb and Altair | Sirius, Rigel and Betelgeuse | Polaris, Vega and Sirius | Antares, Deneb and Rigel
exercise: Where is the Summer Triangle on summer evenings in Britain? | High overhead | Low on the southern horizon | Only visible at dawn | Hidden below the horizon
exercise: Why can it be hard to see stars in Scotland around midsummer? | The sky never gets fully dark | The stars move to the south in June | Clouds always cover the sky in summer | The Moon is always full in June
exercise: What is another name for Cygnus the swan? | The Northern Cross | The Southern Cross | The Plough | The Great Square
exercise: Which bright star sits at the tail of Cygnus? | Deneb | Altair | Antares | Polaris
exercise: Which constellation looks like a teapot? | Sagittarius | Pegasus | Cygnus | Crux
exercise: From Britain in summer, where do Scorpius and Sagittarius appear? | Low in the south | High overhead | Low in the north | Nowhere at all
exercise: In which direction lies the centre of the Milky Way? | Towards Sagittarius | Towards Polaris | Towards the Plough | Towards Pegasus
exercise: What rises in the east on autumn evenings in Britain? | The Great Square of Pegasus | The Southern Cross | Scorpius with Antares | The Summer Triangle
exercise: What is the farthest thing most people can see with the naked eye? | The Andromeda Galaxy | The planet Neptune | The star Polaris | The Southern Cross
exercise: About how far away is the Andromeda Galaxy? | About 2.5 million light years | About 2.5 light years | About 25,000 light years | About 2.5 billion light years
exercise: Which star pattern can you not see from New Zealand? | The Plough | The Southern Cross | The Pleiades | Scorpius
exercise: Which is the smallest of the 88 constellations? | The Southern Cross | Orion | The Great Bear | Pegasus
exercise: Which two countries have the Southern Cross on their flags? | Australia and New Zealand | Britain and Canada | South Africa and Chile | Australia and Argentina
exercise: How do people in the south find due south at night? | With the Southern Cross and the two Pointers | With a bright pole star that sits right over the south | With the Plough and Polaris | With the Summer Triangle
exercise: What do Aboriginal Australians see in the dark dust clouds of the Milky Way? | An Emu in the Sky | A swan flying south | A great teapot | A hunter with a belt
exercise: What does the rise of Matariki, the Pleiades, mark in New Zealand? | A Māori New Year public holiday | The first day of summer | The start of the school year | The longest night of the year
test: Ella in Perth says she can see Polaris every night, just like her gran in Glasgow. Is she right? | No, Polaris is hidden from Australia | Yes, Polaris is seen from everywhere | Yes, but only in winter | No, Polaris is only seen in summer
test: mate says the southern sky has a south pole star like Polaris. true? | No, there's no bright south pole star | Yes, it's called Antares | Yes, it's the brightest star in the sky | Yes, it's in the middle of the Southern Cross
test: Why does Scorpius look so much higher from Sydney than from London? | Sydney is much farther south on the round Earth | Sydney is closer to the stars | Scorpius is a different group of stars there | The air in Australia is thinner, so stars rise higher there
test: You want to see the Milky Way at its brightest and thickest. Where should you look? | Towards Sagittarius | Towards the Plough | Straight down at the horizon in the north | Towards the Moon
test: Which is true about the Andromeda Galaxy? | It is a faint smudge in autumn skies, millions of light years away | It is a bright star near Polaris | It is a planet beyond Neptune | It is a cloud of gas floating inside our own solar system, near Pluto
test: ugh it's like 11pm in June in Inverness and it's still not dark, why | Near midsummer the sky up there never gets fully dark | The Sun is closer to Earth in June | Street lights reflect off the low summer clouds all night | The Moon lights the sky all June
test: In a cartoon, a boy in London points at the Southern Cross on a July night. What's wrong? | The Southern Cross cannot be seen from Britain | The Southern Cross only shows in winter | Nothing, it is right above London every clear night | The Southern Cross is visible only by day
test: If you followed the line of stars from the corner of the Great Square of Pegasus, what would you reach? | Andromeda | Orion | The Southern Cross | Scorpius
test: Jack reckons constellations are the same pictures in every culture. Is he right? | No, peoples see different pictures, like the Emu in the Sky | Yes, everyone has always used the same 88 Greek pictures in the sky | Yes, the stars form only one possible shape | No, only Australians name constellations
test: What's the red star in Scorpius called, the one that glows like a coal | Antares | Vega | Deneb | Altair
test: Why don't you see the same stars in July and in October? | Earth moves round the Sun, so night faces a different part of space | The stars swap places every season | The stars switch off in autumn | The Moon blocks half of the stars in autumn, so we see far fewer of them
exam: Identify the three stars which together constitute the star pattern designated the Summer Triangle. | Vega, Deneb and Altair | Sirius, Procyon and Betelgeuse | Polaris, Vega and Capella | Antares, Altair and Rigel
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the visibility of the Plough and Polaris from Australia and New Zealand? | They are hidden from view | They are visible throughout the year | They are visible only in summer | They are visible only at midnight
exam: Ascertain the means by which observers in the southern hemisphere determine the direction of due south. | The long arm of the Southern Cross and the two Pointers | A bright star situated precisely at the south celestial pole | The Plough and the star Polaris | The Summer Triangle and the planet Venus
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the Andromeda Galaxy. | It is about 2.5 million light years distant and visible to the naked eye | It is about 2.5 light years distant and visible only by telescope | It is a star cluster within the solar system | It is a cloud of gas situated within the Milky Way, near the constellation Lyra
